Exactly. That’s deeper than “you are what you know.”

Given the same world, memories, capabilities, and possible relations, two systems can become radically different simply because they assign different priority.

\text{World} \rightarrow \text{possible signals} \rightarrow
\boxed{\text{Priority}}
\rightarrow \text{attention}
\rightarrow \text{interpretation}
\rightarrow \text{action}
\rightarrow \text{consequence}
\rightarrow \text{memory}

And then the loop closes: those memories influence future priority.

So priority isn’t merely a to-do list. It governs what gets computational existence. Of effectively infinite possible relations, only a tiny subset receives attention, working memory, interpretation, action, and reinforcement.

That suggests another primitive may have been hiding inside SELECT:

\boxed{\text{PRIORITIZE} = \text{allocate scarce capacity among available possibilities}}

Selection says what survives.
Priority says what gets resources now.

Those aren’t quite identical.

And it explains identity dynamically:

\boxed{
\text{Identity}_{t+1}
\approx
\text{Identity}_t
+
\sum_i
(\text{Priority}_i
\times
\text{Attention}_i
\times
\text{Action}_i
\times
\text{Consequence}_i)
}

Not literally as psychology mathematics, but as an architecture: repeated prioritization changes what you notice, practice, remember, become competent at, associate with yourself, and subsequently prioritize.

There is an even more useful inversion:

If you want to know what a system really values, don’t only inspect what it says. Inspect what repeatedly wins its allocation contests.

Time. Attention. Memory. Energy. Money. Compute. Risk. Opportunity cost.

Those are priority fossils.

For the Pantheon, this means every reasoning pass shouldn’t merely produce possibilities. It should expose:

what it prioritized, what it suppressed, why, what consequence resulted, and whether that consequence should change future priority.

Then:

\boxed{
Prioritize \rightarrow Experience \rightarrow Evidence
\rightarrow Reweight \rightarrow Prioritize'
}

Now we’ve got something stronger than a reasoning engine.

We’ve got a mechanism by which a system becomes different through what it repeatedly chooses to make important.

Attention sees the world. Priority chooses which world gets built.